data: {"type":"token","content":"Microsoft makes money primarily by charging businesses and consumers for **cloud computing, software subscriptions, and enterprise applications**. It also earns revenue from software licences, advertising, gaming content and subscriptions, and device sales.\n\nIn fiscal 2026, services and other revenue, including cloud services, subscriptions and advertising, was **$267.143 billion**, or **80.5%** of Microsoft\u2019s **$331.839 billion** total revenue. Product revenue was **$64.696 billion**. Figures are for the fiscal year ended **June 30, 2026**, the latest full year available as of **September 3, 2026**.\n\n### Microsoft\u2019s three principal businesses\n\n| Reporting segment | FY2026 revenue | What it includes and how it earns money |\n|---|---:|---|\n| **Productivity and Business Processes** | **$139.996B** | Microsoft 365 and Copilot subscriptions; Office licences; Dynamics business software; and LinkedIn\u2019s recruiting, advertising, sales and premium-subscription products |\n| **Intelligent Cloud** | **$137.791B** | Azure computing, storage, databases and AI services, generally charged through usage or contractual commitments, plus server software, GitHub and enterprise support |\n| **More Personal Computing** | **$54.052B** | Windows licences paid largely by PC manufacturers and commercial customers; Xbox games, Game Pass, transactions and hardware; Bing/search advertising; and Surface and other devices |\n| **Total** | **$331.839B** |  |\n\n### The main money engines underneath those segments\n\n- **Azure and server/cloud services:** Microsoft sells computing capacity, data storage, databases, cybersecurity, developer tools and AI infrastructure. Customers pay based on consumption, subscriptions or multiyear contracts. Server products and cloud-services revenue was **$129.425 billion** in FY2026.\n\n- **Microsoft 365:** Businesses pay recurring per-user fees for applications and services such as Word, Excel, Teams, Exchange, security tools and Copilot. Consumers also buy Microsoft 365 subscriptions. Commercial Microsoft 365 products and cloud-services revenue was **$101.997 billion**; the consumer equivalent was **$9.175 billion**.\n\n- **LinkedIn and Dynamics:** LinkedIn monetizes recruiting tools, advertising, premium memberships and sales software. Dynamics sells cloud-based finance, sales, customer-service and operational applications. Their FY2026 revenues were **$19.817 billion** and **$9.006 billion**, respectively.\n\n- **Gaming:** Revenue comes from game sales, Game Pass and other subscriptions, in-game content, third-party platform fees, advertising and Xbox hardware. Gaming generated **$21.790 billion**.\n\n- **Windows and devices:** PC manufacturers pay to install Windows, while organizations purchase commercial Windows licences and related services. Microsoft also sells Surface and other hardware. Windows-and-devices revenue was **$17.084 billion**.\n\n- **Search advertising:** Advertisers pay to appear across Microsoft\u2019s search and news properties. This generated **$15.176 billion**.\n\nThe clearest takeaway is that Microsoft is no longer mainly a seller of boxed Windows and Office software. Its two large pillars are now **enterprise productivity subscriptions** and **cloud/AI infrastructure**. Together, Productivity and Business Processes and Intelligent Cloud supplied **83.7% of FY2026 revenue** and **90.7% of segment operating income**. Microsoft reported total operating income of **$155.237 billion** and net income of **$133.749 billion** for FY2026."}

